## 15. Demonstration Scenarios and Testing

Use Phase 3 analytical findings and Phase 4 alerts to test the Phase 5 recommendation workflow.

| Phase 3 / Phase 4 input | Expected Phase 5 behaviour |
|---|---|
| Advisory environmental finding | Create a Caution-level preventive recommendation for the astronaut. |
| Warning environmental finding with normal health readings | Create a Warning-level recommendation and a ground medical team escalation record. |
| Critical environmental finding | Create a Critical recommendation, prominent astronaut guidance and a high-priority ground medical team escalation record. |
| Advisory health finding with normal environmental readings | Create a Caution-level preventive recommendation for the astronaut. |
| Warning health finding | Create a Warning-level recommendation and a ground medical team escalation record. |
| Combined health and environmental finding | Display both relevant sets of readings and the possible relationship identified by Phase 3. |
| Mild hydration, nutrition, fatigue or recovery concern | Provide general preventive guidance without diagnosing or prescribing medication. |
| Phase 3 finding changes from Warning to Critical | Update the existing recommendation to Critical rather than creating a duplicate. |
| Phase 3 reports that a concern has resolved | Update the corresponding recommendation status and preserve its history. |
| Astronaut changes location | Keep previous recommendations associated with the original location. |
| Phase 3 report has missing or unreliable data | Display the uncertainty and do not invent missing information. |
| Communication is unavailable | Store the recommendation locally, queue it for transmission and display the offline or queued status. |
| Communication becomes available | Transmit queued recommendations, alerts and records according to the queue status. |
| Heart-rate sensor stops sending readings | Detect a sensor failure or data gap, mark the readings as unavailable, notify the astronaut and avoid treating the missing heart rate as a normal reading. |
| SpO2 sensor sends incomplete or inconsistent values | Detect inconsistent or incomplete data, mark the readings as unreliable and display the data-quality limitation. |
| Temperature sensor sends the same reading continuously | Detect a repeated-value or frozen-reading issue and mark the sensor as degraded or inconsistent. |
| Wearable device disconnects | Detect the disconnection, display the sensor as disconnected and preserve the last valid reading with its original timestamp. |
| Sensor battery becomes low | Display a Low Battery sensor status and notify the astronaut before the sensor becomes unavailable. |
| Data transmission is interrupted | Detect the interruption, queue or store affected data locally and display the transmission status. |
| Safety-critical sensor fails | Warn the astronaut about reduced monitoring coverage, recommend fallback procedures and escalate when required. |
| Sensor recovers after a failure | Update the sensor status, resume normal monitoring and preserve the complete failure and recovery history. |

Verify that the recommendations use the exact readings, classifications, explanations, timestamps, alert statuses and recommended actions supplied by Phase 3 and Phase 4.

