## 18. Data Accuracy and Safety

- Clearly label simulated readings, findings, alerts and recommendations as SIMULATED DATA.
- Never present simulated readings as actual astronaut measurements.
- Never claim physical sensors are connected.
- Never claim real ground medical personnel, emergency services or mission control have been contacted.
- Do not invent missing readings, analytical findings or alerts.
- Do not independently diagnose medical conditions.
- Do not prescribe medication.
- Do not present possible environmental-health relationships as proven causes.
- Do not describe demonstration risk classifications or severity levels as clinically validated.
- Preserve uncertainty and sensor reliability information supplied by Phase 3.
- Do not replace qualified medical personnel or approved mission procedures.
- For non-trivial concerns, direct astronauts to approved procedures, onboard medical personnel or ground medical support.
- For trivial concerns, provide only general preventive guidance and clearly distinguish it from medical treatment.
- Do not treat a missing, delayed, inconsistent or unreliable sensor reading as a normal reading.
- Do not automatically interpret a sensor failure as a medical emergency or environmental hazard.
- Do not invent replacement readings for failed or unavailable sensors.
- Clearly distinguish sensor failures from actual health or environmental concerns.
- Clearly label sensor-health statuses, failure alerts and recovery updates as SIMULATED DATA.

